10
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:00,790
Awesome.

11
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:04,950
Well, is there anything else about your background that you would like to bring up before we dive into our topic today?

12
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:06,630
No, I think that's great to get started.

13
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,710
I love technology.

14
00:01:08,710 --> 00:01:10,230
I love human behavior and I love building things.

15
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:16,550
Brilliant. Brilliant. [clears throat] So, today we will be talking about ambiguity in human input.

16
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:23,830
Before we get into the specific details, let's go ahead and introduce the problem of ambiguity to our listeners.

17
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:29,590
So when we talk about ambiguity in human input, what do you actually mean?

18
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,510
Yeah.

19
00:01:31,510 --> 00:01:40,710
In general, when I think of ambiguity, I think of it as when something could mean two or more different things and it's genuinely not possible to figure out what's the case.

20
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:51,830
And so this is the case, for example, if I'm communicating with another person, I might say something slightly cryptically and the person listening might think that I'm being ambiguous with respect to what I've communicated.

21
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:53,910
And it can also be true for machine learning systems.

22
00:01:53,910 --> 00:02:03,830
If you don't give them enough data or enough context, it might be really hard to figure out what's actually being communicated or what's actually being conveyed. And so there's some ambiguity involved there.

23
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:08,309
Would you classify noise or error also as ambiguity?

24
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:13,110
I think of ambiguity not as a function of the output of the system.

25
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:16,309
So errors are a function of the output of the system.

26
00:02:16,309 --> 00:02:18,150
Noise is a function of the input of the system.

27
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:21,431
Ambiguity is kind of intrinsic as a property. Right?

28
00:02:21,431 --> 00:02:25,110
So if you're communicating something and you haven't given all the context or

29
00:02:25,110 --> 00:02:36,790
given all of the information that's inherently ambiguous and by adding more context, adding more inputs, removing more noise, I could get things to have less ambiguity.

30
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:43,030
And so when things are ambiguous, what happens is it's easy to have an error because it's possible to interpret things one of two ways.

31
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:47,350
So it's highly related to noise, it's highly related to errors, but I think of it a little bit differently.

32
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:50,229
I see.

33
00:02:50,229 --> 00:02:55,590
As I seem to understand it, ambiguity itself feels like an ambiguous problem. Can you expand on that?

34
00:02:55,590 --> 00:03:01,509
How does ambiguity even by definition feel solvable?

35
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:04,070
Yeah.

36
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:09,670
So things are ambiguous when it comes to communication and when it comes to real world interactions usually because of a lack of context, right?

37
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:19,430
So, if I say that's great and I have a like sight inflection that's paused at the end of it versus that's great, that can mean two very very different things.

38
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:27,750
And if I just saw the words that's great, it's really hard to know which of those it could be because it could in reality be either of them.

39
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:31,910
And so the kind of only way to resolve ambiguity is with more information, right?

40
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:35,830
So if you just had the words, you wouldn't be able to tell what I meant.

41
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:41,030
But if you have extra context, for example, in that case, my tone and my inflection, you might be able to figure it out.

42
00:03:41,030 --> 00:03:42,630
You could also figure it out lots of other ways.

43
00:03:42,630 --> 00:03:46,309
If you know what I was saying just before it or after it, so on and so forth.

44
00:03:46,319 --> 00:03:56,949
So things like inflection points and context, they seem to help humans quite a lot in resolving ambiguity in conversations.

45
00:03:56,949 --> 00:03:59,190
So context, structure, things like that are very obvious to us.

46
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,789
Yeah.

47
00:04:02,789 --> 00:04:05,110
Why is it so intrinsically hard for machine learning models?

48
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:10,869
It's hard for machine learning models because they're only given a small set of information at a point in time.

49
00:04:10,879 --> 00:04:14,070
Right? So, we're having this conversation.

50
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:20,949
You've now already built up the context of the last 3 or 4 minutes of what we've talked about to make sense of every single statement I'm making going forward.

51
00:04:20,949 --> 00:04:26,550
But most machine learning models, they're just given a little bit of information at a point in time.

52
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:28,470
They do a task and then they forget about what's happened. Right?

53
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:37,990
Think about every interaction you have with all these LLMs and AI systems and how they learn a thing in one conversation and then forget it the moment after.

54
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:42,790
And so that's where people do a really good job at resolving uncertainty and ambiguity.

55
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:45,430
And these ML systems have a much harder time doing it.

56
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:52,310
We will get more into the details of exactly how machine learning models do that.

57
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:54,870
But can we talk about the problem you're solving at Whisper?

58
00:04:54,870 --> 00:04:57,909
What does a voice first input system need to handle?

59
00:04:57,919 --> 00:04:59,909
Yeah.

60
00:04:59,909 --> 00:05:05,110
So, our goal at Whisper is to build the first voice interface that a billion people can rely on as their primary interaction with our computers.

61
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:09,350
And we're starting by solving for the thing that people spend most of their time doing, which is communicating.

62
00:05:09,350 --> 00:05:11,590
We type a lot, hundreds of times a day.

63
00:05:11,590 --> 00:05:17,670
And if you could speak to communicate to your computer, it makes interacting way more effortless.

64
00:05:17,670 --> 00:05:21,029
The challenge is that when we speak, there's all sorts of ambiguity.

65
00:05:21,029 --> 00:05:26,390
Sometimes you might be mumbling into a microphone and there's a fire truck going by in the background.

66
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,870
Sometimes you might correct yourself midway through a sentence.

67
00:05:30,870 --> 00:05:39,830
Sometimes we might not know whether you want something to be more readable as a list or

68
00:05:39,830 --> 00:05:44,070
be a semistructured thought dump depending on who you're communicating with.

69
00:05:44,070 --> 00:05:50,310
And so the ambiguity comes from not fully being able to understand what a person says, not fully being able to understand how they might want it,

70
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:58,150
etc. And if you've ever used a voice system like Siri or Alexa and it's ever misunderstood you, sometimes those cases are actually hard.

71
00:05:58,150 --> 00:06:01,029
Sometimes they're not and sometimes it should have just gotten it right.

72
00:06:01,029 --> 00:06:05,909
But sometimes they're actually hard and those are the kinds of cases of ambiguity that we're focused on solving here at Whisper.

73
00:06:05,919 --> 00:06:10,309
I feel like we all have experiences like that with S.

74
00:06:10,309 --> 00:06:21,430
[laughter] So let's move on to where the sort of ambiguity shows up and talk about some types of ambiguity when we speak specifically about input.

75
00:06:21,430 --> 00:06:26,629
What are the general categories of ambiguity that we would encounter?

76
00:06:26,629 --> 00:06:32,710
For example, let's think about something like sentence structure as you mentioned within context meanings of words.

77
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:35,430
Can you talk more about these different categories?

78
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:38,790
Yeah, absolutely.

79
00:06:38,790 --> 00:06:40,790
The ambiguity comes in a couple of different ways.

80
00:06:40,790 --> 00:06:45,830
So the first one is the style of how you want things written.

81
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:49,909
So for example, think about somebody who's in Gen Z who's using this to text a friend.

82
00:06:49,909 --> 00:06:54,950
Like the way that people say things out loud and the way they write their text to their friends look very, very different.

83
00:06:54,950 --> 00:06:59,510
That same person might also be using this to talk to their boss at work.

84
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:06,070
And the way that they speak might still be the same because we tend to speak the same way, but the way that that has to be written is going to look different.

85
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:11,029
And so ambiguity here or preferences come from a variety of different ways.

86
00:07:11,029 --> 00:07:15,830
There's a preference of how you want things formatted and written.

87
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:25,830
How you want it to be cleaned up from the way we speak, which is in lots and lots of run-on sentences into a much more precise and articulate version of what you're saying.

88
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:31,909
Or even really simple things like people start words or sentences with things like but or though or like.

89
00:07:31,919 --> 00:07:39,270
And often the reason they do that is it makes a message seem a lot more casual and informal.

90
00:07:39,270 --> 00:07:42,790
And so in like Slack or iMessage, I might want that to be preserved.

91
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:52,950
But if I'm writing a really professional email to somebody, then I actually want it to be cleaned up so that I can go from the way that I sound with my unstructured thoughts to a really polished version of myself.

92
00:07:52,950 --> 00:08:03,430
So, what I'm hearing is not only does the context of what a user is saying matters, but also what the user actually wants, what their intent is.

93
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:05,270
That's right. Where they're trying to convey.

94
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:08,230
That's right. And that's the kind of context that most systems don't have.

95
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:16,469
The only way to figure it out is am I messaging my wife, like my co-founder, a person on my team, like my mom?

96
00:08:16,469 --> 00:08:20,230
I'm going to do a different thing in each of those cases.

97
00:08:20,230 --> 00:08:22,469
And there's no way to guess that unless you have that kind of information.

98
00:08:22,479 --> 00:08:30,070
I would only imagine that this ambiguity also shows up in thoughts beyond text.

99
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:38,070
So think of, for example, accents or background noise, somebody talking very far away from the mic.

100
00:08:38,070 --> 00:08:42,871
Would you consider all of these as under the same umbrella? And how would you solve that?

101
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:44,710
Absolutely.

102
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:46,710
I just think of all of it as intrinsic uncertainty.

103
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:48,630
So what's the uncertainty that you have?

104
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:57,670
And the case of accents, for example, with somebody with a heavy accent saying two or three words, if you don't know their accent up front, it's actually very hard sometimes to tell what language they're speaking.

105
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:08,230
And if you know their voice, if you know the history of how they speak and what their intonation sounds like, it's actually much easier to figure out what they're saying.

106
00:09:08,230 --> 00:09:13,750
And so in the case of people who have different types of accents, knowing their accent up front itself helps.

107
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:22,070
In the case of somebody who's far away from a microphone and might not be speaking too clearly, then knowing the topic that you're speaking about might help quite a bit.

108
00:09:22,070 --> 00:09:27,190
Or knowing the person's voice and what it normally sounds like so you can pick it out from the background noise.

109
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:28,870
That might also help quite a bit.

110
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:33,750
And so there's lots of different sources that you could use to understand this and it's how we understand each other, right?

111
00:09:33,750 --> 00:09:45,509
Maybe my favorite example of this is like whenever I meet somebody with a new accent, for the first couple minutes, it's kind of hard for me to understand what it is they're saying and after two or three minutes of getting to know them, it gets a lot easier very fast.

112
00:09:45,519 --> 00:09:52,790
And that's the kind of way in which we learn about how to understand different people's accents in different situations.

113
00:09:52,790 --> 00:09:57,030
And our goal when we're building our technology is to do the same thing for people.

114
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:04,630
So as humans we are able to associate different accents and

115
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:11,750
what words mean in that accent and then take that into consideration when we hear it again after a certain amount of time is when

116
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:18,069
you talk about machine learning models are they also doing the same thing how are they able to

117
00:10:18,069 --> 00:10:24,150
take in context and keep that within context while taking in more input as time goes on.

118
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:26,470
Yeah.

119
00:10:26,470 --> 00:10:30,949
So, by default, these systems aren't great at that, which is the reason why they make lots of mistakes that feel stupid and silly.

120
00:10:30,949 --> 00:10:34,790
They're obvious to us, but they're not obvious to these systems.

121
00:10:34,790 --> 00:10:41,110
And this is where LLMs have been really powerful because LLM, the whole discipline has been around context engineering.

122
00:10:41,110 --> 00:10:42,871
How do you get the context? How do you supply to the LLM?

123
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:47,670
How do you help it make sense of more stuff and make better decisions?

124
00:10:47,670 --> 00:10:54,630
And one of the core research problems we're solving here at Whisper is how do you give that kind of context to a speech model or an audio model?

125
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:59,509
Right now the audio models that most people use don't take this context into account.

126
00:10:59,509 --> 00:11:09,030
And so if we can solve for that at a kind of fundamental level then we can unlock this capability in a way that works in not just the text domain but also other domains.

127
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:20,389
So you would want to as a first pass want to solve this problem by tackling the lowest common denominator and building a generalized solution that works for most cases.

128
00:11:20,389 --> 00:11:28,389
How would you go about building one system for all users in our for example conversation when we're talking with different accents?

129
00:11:28,399 --> 00:11:35,030
How are you able to build a single model that takes into consideration all of those things?

130
00:11:35,030 --> 00:11:38,389
Let me think of an example here.

131
00:11:38,389 --> 00:11:45,350
Let's say I have a lot of people with very interesting long names or who use jargon a lot with acronyms.

132
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:47,110
Systems often get these wrong.

133
00:11:47,110 --> 00:11:49,110
What is the impact here?

134
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:51,829
How would you build a singular system that tackles this problem?

135
00:11:51,839 --> 00:11:57,670
I would think of it the same way as how people prompt LLMs.

136
00:11:57,670 --> 00:12:05,829
So if you're for example coding and you say can you update this function, then if the LLM has access to your codebase, it knows exactly what to do for you.

137
00:12:05,829 --> 00:12:09,509
And that's a fully general system that works for each person.

138
00:12:09,509 --> 00:12:11,509
And so the same thing, the same idea could be applied to a speech model.

139
00:12:11,519 --> 00:12:15,590
All of these systems are getting unified.

140
00:12:15,590 --> 00:12:17,750
We're putting speech into LLM backbones.

141
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:28,949
And then if you can give it all of this information as a prompt, as information over time, suppose we know the set of acronyms that you've used in the past that you've fixed your output to in the past, then we can use that again going forward.

142
00:12:28,959 --> 00:12:41,350
So is there a distinction between inherent ambiguity where no right answer really exists versus ambiguity that is reducible where more context can help?

143
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:43,350
Can you talk a little more about that?

144
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:47,509
Yeah, maybe an example of that is the question you just asked.

145
00:12:47,509 --> 00:12:53,670
So, whenever there's ambiguity in what somebody's saying, usually people ask clarifying or follow-up questions.

146
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:57,670
There was some intrinsic uncertainty, some intrinsic unknown in what they just said.

147
00:12:57,670 --> 00:13:04,790
And so, people ask a follow-up question and they might ask another follow-up question and another follow-up question afterwards because the topic's confusing.

148
00:13:04,790 --> 00:13:07,430
It doesn't make sense, right?

149
00:13:07,430 --> 00:13:09,590
And so, a lot of times there's inherent uncertainty.

150
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:15,030
The world is full of so much inherent uncertainty like we can't predict the future, right?

151
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:16,950
And so we reveal it one step at a time. We ask questions.

152
00:13:16,950 --> 00:13:19,430
We clarify things that can help.

153
00:13:19,430 --> 00:13:28,949
And so at the end of the day, even with what we're building, one of the ways in which we can continue to improve it is if we're not sure what you said, what if we just asked you, did you say this thing or did you say this other thing?

154
00:13:28,949 --> 00:13:31,829
That's what we do when we don't hear another person when we're with them live.

155
00:13:31,839 --> 00:13:38,230
Does that introduce friction in the user experience?

156
00:13:38,230 --> 00:13:39,990
Certainly, which is why you have to use it sparingly, right?

157
00:13:39,990 --> 00:13:44,550
If I was talking to you and after every single answer I gave you, you said, "Could you say that again?"

158
00:13:44,550 --> 00:13:47,990
I would be very annoyed if talking to you very quickly.

159
00:13:47,990 --> 00:13:49,670
Like, I wouldn't want to be on this call.

160
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:51,269
But if you asked me that once or twice, could you say that again?

161
00:13:51,269 --> 00:13:52,870
I didn't quite get it.

162
00:13:52,870 --> 00:13:54,629
I'd be okay with it, right?

163
00:13:54,629 --> 00:13:59,590
Because I know that that's how I can feel confident that you've understood me.

164
00:13:59,590 --> 00:14:03,189
And so, that's where it's just important to navigate the trade-offs really carefully here.

165
00:14:03,189 --> 00:14:08,389
What about situations where, and I'm just spitballing here,

166
00:14:08,389 --> 00:14:17,990
I say a sentence that I want you to understand and you keep asking me to repeat the same sentence over and over again because you won't get it.

167
00:14:17,990 --> 00:14:19,910
Do you find this a common experience? And how would you deal with it?

168
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:30,790
With us, it's pretty rare for what we're building because usually if you speak a little bit more clearly or maybe the issue is that you have the wrong microphone connected, like we can help you figure out why it's not working.

169
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:39,670
When it's a person communicating with another person, if you don't understand it though and you keep just saying the same question over and over again back to a person saying, "Can you just state it again?"

170
00:14:39,670 --> 00:14:41,509
You're probably never going to make it anywhere, right?

171
00:14:41,509 --> 00:14:44,150
Instead, you want to say, "Hey, I didn't understand it that way that you said it.

172
00:14:44,150 --> 00:14:46,790
Like, could you try explaining it to me a different way?"

173
00:14:46,790 --> 00:14:49,990
Or, "Could you try explaining it to me like I'm five or something of that sort?"

174
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:56,870
And so, the idea here is to think about it as how would you as a person actually try to navigate this issue? How would you try to clarify it?

175
00:14:56,870 --> 00:14:58,389
How would you try to make sense of it?

176
00:14:58,389 --> 00:15:01,110
And to focus exactly on that.

177
00:15:01,110 --> 00:15:07,829
I'm thinking of an example of swiping on a keyboard to form a word and when you swipe incorrectly or

178
00:15:07,829 --> 00:15:14,069
you swipe and it is the same signature as another word and

179
00:15:14,069 --> 00:15:23,430
you press the backspace button you doing the same action again gives you a new recommendation for a word that is similar.

180
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:25,189
Is it something like that that we're thinking of here?

181
00:15:25,199 --> 00:15:28,710
Exactly. That's right. Awesome.

182
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:30,230
This seems like a very interesting problem.

183
00:15:30,230 --> 00:15:39,990
So I really want to pivot now to talking about the training of these models and what the architectures look like for handling ambiguity within context.

184
00:15:39,990 --> 00:15:45,430
So I'd imagine you would want to build a generally robust system at a model training level.

185
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:53,189
How would you provide the additional context that is required to reduce ambiguity?

186
00:15:53,189 --> 00:16:00,949
If you want to, for example, take into consideration a person's tone or their accent,

187
00:16:00,949 --> 00:16:06,069
how would you include those kinds of metadata or additional signals into the model training process?

188
00:16:06,079 --> 00:16:15,350
Yeah, a good example of how we might do that is the very last thing that you said probably influences the next thing that you're going to say.

189
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:20,629
And so as part of training these models, we can always give it a prompt, which is the last thing that you said, right?

190
00:16:20,629 --> 00:16:25,030
And use that as information that helps you figure out what the next thing is.

191
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:30,629
And that prompt might even include the audio clip of your voice or some kind of representation of your voice.

192
00:16:30,629 --> 00:16:40,389
And so by training that into the model, then it learns how to in the cases where things are hard, make sense of that kind of information and use it to resolve the uncertainty.

193
00:16:40,389 --> 00:16:46,710
But how would you actually go about constructing a data set that captures all of this information?

194
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:57,829
So an example of this, if you look at a lot of open source audio data sets, open source audio data sets have many different speakers in it, each of whom say many different things.

195
00:16:57,829 --> 00:17:03,829
And so one way of doing this, a simple way would be, okay, great.

