00:35:51,829 --> 00:35:59,349
So when we talk in two languages from in one sentence that is still being embedded into the same space as one language.

383
00:35:59,349 --> 00:36:02,630
So you're right in saying that's a third new language in itself.

384
00:36:02,640 --> 00:36:07,589
So we spoke about personalization and learning from user behavior.

385
00:36:07,589 --> 00:36:09,670
Let's talk about the user experience a little more.

386
00:36:09,680 --> 00:36:18,550
How do we communicate uncertainty with users without degrading their experience using these models?

387
00:36:18,560 --> 00:36:30,630
I think that's generally a pretty hard question cuz people don't really like being told that they're not sure about something like that's um it's not what people want.

388
00:36:30,630 --> 00:36:35,910
I think generally the way I think about it is like think about when you're typing on a keyboard you see three autocorrect suggestions.

389
00:36:35,910 --> 00:36:40,950
That kind of user experience is not bad because it's like, oh, maybe it's one of these three things.

390
00:36:40,950 --> 00:36:47,430
They can click a button and fix it when there's uncertainty, but it presents certainty in the text box, right?

391
00:36:47,430 --> 00:36:54,550
Like you got the full full thing that you typed out and you just got a couple buttons you can tap to fix it if you need to. That's how I tend to think about that.

392
00:36:54,560 --> 00:36:58,470
Giving users too many choices leads to more confusion.

393
00:36:58,480 --> 00:36:59,990
Exactly.

394
00:36:59,990 --> 00:37:03,191
If they can go fix it afterwards, they can go fix it afterwards. It's frustrating.

395
00:37:03,191 --> 00:37:06,710
It's a little paper cut that you're getting as a user, but it's solvable.

396
00:37:06,720 --> 00:37:20,310
So, if I am saying a sentence that includes uncommon names or acronyms, would you rather have the system interpret it incorrectly and have the user correct it?

397
00:37:20,320 --> 00:37:23,670
I think that's probably the first thing that happens, right?

398
00:37:23,670 --> 00:37:27,030
Where once they do that once, then I don't want to make that mistake again from our end, right?

399
00:37:27,040 --> 00:37:29,270
That's how we work on that.

400
00:37:29,270 --> 00:37:37,829
And I think the other part of it is sometimes people will just say like oh spelled a m e y for example and

401
00:37:37,829 --> 00:37:41,990
framing things that way just however people will naturally try to communicate that

402
00:37:41,990 --> 00:37:49,910
when there's a little bit of uncertainty in the spelling we just have to handle that for you.

403
00:37:49,910 --> 00:38:00,790
So that seems like a graceful fall back in design to be able to tell the user, hey, if you think that a name that you're going to say is going to be hard for me to decipher, spell it out for me.

404
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:05,910
It's the same way that we do if we're talking on the phone with somebody and we want to make sure they get it right.

405
00:38:05,920 --> 00:38:10,470
Exactly. It's helpful to learn the natural alphabet then.

406
00:38:10,470 --> 00:38:18,390
[laughter] So recommendation systems often show multiple options because they don't know any user preference initially.

407
00:38:18,390 --> 00:38:22,710
Should text and voice systems be doing the same thing generally speaking?

408
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:27,349
No. I I tend to think of all of this as mirroring human interactions.

409
00:38:27,349 --> 00:38:34,230
Like if you said the same thing four different ways before I had a chance to try to understand what you were saying,

410
00:38:34,230 --> 00:38:40,630
like that would be a kind of painful interaction between the two of us, right?

411
00:38:40,630 --> 00:38:49,030
or like whenever I get the chatbt UI with two different things like I kind of shudder a little bit inside cuz I'm like now I have to read both of these things and or I don't have to right I I usually just read one of them and

412
00:38:49,030 --> 00:38:59,670
then I'm just kind of confused and overwhelmed honestly speaking which is where it's like that's not where it's useful on on the recommendation side it's as a user I'm trying to explore what I want and

413
00:38:59,670 --> 00:39:09,510
so I'm happy to see multiple things because my goal was to see multiple things if I search for something my goal was to see multiple things and so it's all about fulfilling ing the user promise, right?

414
00:39:09,510 --> 00:39:15,670
If I asked you to find restaurants for us to go to dinner to tonight, you might suggest two or three options.

415
00:39:15,670 --> 00:39:17,270
That way, like I have some choice as well, right?

416
00:39:17,270 --> 00:39:23,990
And so that's how I think about it's all in parallel to how people interact with people.

417
00:39:23,990 --> 00:39:26,069
We want to make that the same way that you interact with devices.

418
00:39:26,079 --> 00:39:33,510
Let's switch gears and talk about resolution and evaluation of these models.

419
00:39:33,510 --> 00:39:38,310
How would you evaluate whether a system that you build is handling ambiguity well enough?

420
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:40,150
There's two parts of it.

421
00:39:40,150 --> 00:39:42,310
One part is consistency, right?

422
00:39:42,310 --> 00:39:46,790
So when things are ambiguous, ideally you want a system to make a consistent decision.

423
00:39:46,790 --> 00:39:51,990
And then the second part is knowing whether more of it could be resolved.

424
00:39:51,990 --> 00:39:56,550
Like more context could help make sense of more of these things.

425
00:39:56,550 --> 00:40:00,390
And like one of the ways you could do this is we we just talked about recommener systems, right?

426
00:40:00,390 --> 00:40:05,109
where it can output five guesses of what you might want.

427
00:40:05,109 --> 00:40:14,710
We could have our systems do the same and we could see, hey, how much inconsistency is there between the top five guesses of what we think is the right answer?

428
00:40:14,710 --> 00:40:18,790
And are we consistently ranking them in some kind of systematic way?

429
00:40:18,790 --> 00:40:26,069
So there's a predictable behavior and there's some level of inconsistency or uncertainty in thinking, oh, maybe it's this or that or that or that.

430
00:40:26,069 --> 00:40:36,790
Is there some kind of output set that you would be measuring results in contrast with to be able to get specific metrics out for systems like this?

431
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:49,190
I think basically the way that we do it is we take the examples where we know the answer and maybe it's just really hard to predict the answer given the input, right?

432
00:40:49,190 --> 00:40:51,670
But we know what the answer is.

433
00:40:51,670 --> 00:40:56,309
And so the level of ambiguity left in the system is how much you still get things wrong given that.

434
00:40:56,309 --> 00:41:07,191
And the measure of how much we're able to reduce that ambiguity is by seeing how much we can get things right by giving more and more context or more intelligence to these models. Right?

435
00:41:07,191 --> 00:41:11,829
Either one of those things can help it.

436
00:41:13,510 --> 00:41:15,750
Looking for a way to stand out in a competitive job market.

437
00:41:15,750 --> 00:41:25,910
Whether you're trying to land your first contributor role or prove you can deliver, take the next step in your career with the new software professional certification from the ITE computer society.

438
00:41:25,920 --> 00:41:33,829
Sign up today at computer.org/certification.

439
00:41:36,870 --> 00:41:43,190
So let's think about online evaluation. What kind of signals from production usage by users tell you whether you're getting things right or not?

440
00:41:43,190 --> 00:41:48,790
Initially we discussed examples where users change the output that you produced for them.

441
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:51,270
That is obviously one case.

442
00:41:51,270 --> 00:41:55,349
Are there other metrics that you can talk about that you you might use?

443
00:41:55,359 --> 00:41:58,950
I think it's things like that and things like engagement, right?

444
00:41:58,950 --> 00:42:00,710
Like if you use it once and then you never try it again.

445
00:42:00,720 --> 00:42:05,990
We probably did a pretty bad job with that first thing.

446
00:42:05,990 --> 00:42:10,230
And if you use it once and then you come back a hundred more times, you probably did okay, right?

447
00:42:10,230 --> 00:42:12,950
At least okay, maybe great.

448
00:42:12,950 --> 00:42:21,190
And so this is like the set of I'd say two major things right how did the person interact with the output itself and

449
00:42:21,190 --> 00:42:25,589
how long do they spend with it you know you have to be careful here right like in chat GPT if

450
00:42:25,599 --> 00:42:36,710
you keep saying no that's not what I meant please try again like 20 times that's also an example of you not doing something right even though the person's engaging multiple times but chances are if

451
00:42:36,710 --> 00:42:41,030
they come back day after day right that's that's one of the best predictors of doing a better job than before.

452
00:42:41,030 --> 00:42:49,589
So it's all about users engaging over and over and us collecting information based on their usage to set up feedback loops.

453
00:42:49,599 --> 00:42:51,511
Right. Exactly.

454
00:42:51,511 --> 00:42:54,870
Those feedback loops do they help improve the model over time?

455
00:42:54,880 --> 00:42:56,870
Yeah.

456
00:42:56,870 --> 00:43:02,310
For it improves the model over time and also more than more importantly than that it tailors the model to you as a user. Right.

457
00:43:02,310 --> 00:43:09,990
So, as we talked about like as you fix more mistakes, we know more and more about how to not make those mistakes for that user.

458
00:43:09,990 --> 00:43:12,069
That might not transfer to another person.

459
00:43:12,069 --> 00:43:21,030
The topics you speak about, the acronyms, the conversational styles, so on and so forth are going to be different for each person, but we can make that pretty consistent.

460
00:43:21,040 --> 00:43:27,349
So, it seems like there are lessons in our discussion that we're having here that generalize beyond just voice processing.

461
00:43:27,359 --> 00:43:30,710
Yeah. Yeah, talking about different types of models.

462
00:43:30,710 --> 00:43:35,349
You work on voice at whisper, but these problems seem to appear everywhere.

463
00:43:35,349 --> 00:43:40,790
Think of chats like chat GPT to co-pilots, recommendation engines.

464
00:43:40,790 --> 00:43:45,510
What type of lessons about ambiguity would you say would transfer across the industry?

465
00:43:45,520 --> 00:43:56,950
I think most of them I think like almost all of these things that I'm talking about are exercises in how deep learning works today and how people reason about things.

466
00:43:56,950 --> 00:44:05,670
Like even if you were to think about ML for biology, I'm sure a lot of these same things apply where if you give these models for biology or for medical reasoning more and more context,

467
00:44:05,680 --> 00:44:10,309
their ability to make good decisions will be better.

468
00:44:10,309 --> 00:44:16,230
And so like maybe the simple one sentence version of this is ambiguity gets resolved with more context.

469
00:44:16,230 --> 00:44:18,230
The more context you can give a system, the better it does.

470
00:44:18,230 --> 00:44:24,230
And if you find that it's not doing nearly as well as you want it to, see if you can give it that context, right?

471
00:44:24,230 --> 00:44:29,349
And that's true for everything we've seen in this era of deep learning, right?

472
00:44:29,349 --> 00:44:35,030
Where the products that do this well become really delightful to use and the products that don't are pretty frustrating.

473
00:44:35,040 --> 00:44:43,670
So we want to build models that model behaviors that humans usually have.

474
00:44:43,680 --> 00:44:47,349
Yep. As well as they can.

475
00:44:47,349 --> 00:44:56,470
There are listeners of ours who would be building chat interfaces or LLM powered applications that might also run into ambiguity related issues.

476
00:44:56,470 --> 00:45:01,829
What are some things that they should start thinking about when it comes to resolving that type of ambiguity?

477
00:45:01,839 --> 00:45:07,670
I think it all comes back to understanding what is it that your users want and how do you get it for them?

478
00:45:07,680 --> 00:45:14,309
Like when it comes to building new products like that, it always starts with what's the problem you're solving?

479
00:45:14,319 --> 00:45:15,910
Why is it important to them?

480
00:45:15,910 --> 00:45:17,829
Then how do we make sure we get that thing to them?

481
00:45:17,839 --> 00:45:26,710
And that itself is an exercise in understanding what people really want like that's the most important problem when building new products.

482
00:45:26,710 --> 00:45:34,150
Once you know that then you can go and solve the technical and product and go to market challenges related to it and keep building from there.

483
00:45:34,150 --> 00:45:45,910
So let's say I am a user who is building something on GitHub right now for code generation and I am struggling with users who are providing me with underspecified prompts.

484
00:45:45,920 --> 00:45:50,470
So a lot of ambiguity in the input itself.

485
00:45:50,470 --> 00:45:56,870
When should I decide that my tool should ask for clarification versus infer it from code context?

486
00:45:56,880 --> 00:46:00,069
I mean the way claude code has done this is absolutely brilliant, right?

487
00:46:00,069 --> 00:46:03,910
Because it asks just just the right number of questions.

488
00:46:03,910 --> 00:46:13,829
I think there's parts of it where it's a trade-off where one you first figure out can I guess it automatically based on the things that

489
00:46:13,829 --> 00:46:20,870
I have access to and then if if the model's unconfident in that is when it's time to ask questions and then it's about rank ordering and

490
00:46:20,870 --> 00:46:28,550
prioritizing what questions to ask like you only get so much patience from a user in coding settings maybe you get two or three questions that

491
00:46:28,550 --> 00:46:37,589
you can ask somebody and so then it's about ranking ranking the most important ones, the ones that are going to affect the outcome the most and asking just those things.

492
00:46:37,599 --> 00:46:47,910
There are more workflows like GSD and Ralph that go into details on how to resolve this sort of ambiguity, but this has been very helpful.

493
00:46:47,910 --> 00:46:50,950
I thank you so much for your time today.

